FAQ = [
 ("What are the best running boards for a 2016–2023 Toyota Tacoma?",
  "Westin's HDX Stainless Drop 56-127752 is the best overall pick for a Double Cab. It is 401 stainless steel under a textured black powder coat, bolts on with no drilling and carries a 5-year limited warranty. Go Rhino's RB20 is the best flat running board, with a 7.5 in step at the front and galvanized 16-gauge steel. Westin's XTS 42-14205 is the rock slider for trucks that see rocks, Tyger's Star Armor is the rated value pick at 500 lb per side, and TAC's 4.25 in oval bar is the Access Cab pick. The cab decides the part: Double Cab steps do not fit the Access Cab, and a wheel-to-wheel bar such as Westin's 56-534185 must also match the 73.7 in bed."),
 ("Do Double Cab running boards fit a Tacoma Access Cab?",
  "No. A cab-length step is cut to the door opening, and its brackets are spaced for one cab. Toyota's 2023 release describes two cab types, the extended Access Cab and the four-door Double Cab. Tyger says on its Star Armor page that the Double Cab part only fits the cab with four full-size doors and is not for the Access Cab, which has two full-size front doors and two rear-hinged doors. TAC sells a separate bar whose title names the 2005–2023 Access Cab, and that is the Access Cab pick on this page. Count the doors, then read the title for the cab name. If the title names no cab, ask the seller."),
 ("Which 2016–2023 Tacoma grades came with factory running boards?",
  "We could not confirm this from a Toyota source. Toyota's 2023 Tacoma release does not mention running boards, side steps, tube steps or rock rails on any grade or package, and Wikipedia's Tacoma page does not mention them for this generation. Steps can also be added by a dealer or a previous owner, which no grade list shows. The reliable check is the truck itself: look under the rocker panel for a bar and brackets, or read the window sticker and the accessory section of the owner's manual. If steps are fitted, an aftermarket set replaces them, so ask the seller whether the new brackets use the same mounting points."),
 ("Do 2005–2015 Tacoma running boards fit a 2016–2023 Tacoma?",
  "It depends on the part, so go by the maker's fit list. Westin lists the HDX Stainless Drop 56-127752 and the XTS Rock Slider 42-14205 for the 2005–2023 Tacoma Double Cab, which covers both generations with one part number. Tyger lists Star Armor for 2005–2023, and TAC lists its Access Cab bar for 2005–2023. Not every part carries over. RealTruck lists the VoltStep power board 20442987PC for 2016–2023 only, and Westin lists the wheel-to-wheel HDX Drop 56-534185 for 2016–2023 only. If you are moving a used set from an older truck, look up its part number first. Do not assume the years."),
 ("Do 2016–2023 Tacoma running boards fit the 2024 and newer Tacoma?",
  "Treat them as different parts. Westin's fit lines for the parts on this page stop at 2023, and the listing titles we found show separate part numbers for the newer truck: Westin's Outlaw drop bar is 20-12775 for the 2005–2023 Double Cab and 20-12785 for 2024–2026, and Go Rhino's RB20 kit is 69442987 for the 05-23 Double Cab and 69443280 for the 2024 Double Cab. Wikipedia says the 2024 truck uses the TNGA-F platform and comes as an XtraCab or a Double Cab. If you drive the newer truck, use our 2024–2026 Tacoma guide and buy only a listing that names 2024 or later."),
 ("Are rock sliders better than running boards on a Tacoma?",
  "They do different jobs. A running board or nerf bar is a step, and it bolts on to make the climb easier. A rock slider is built to keep rocks off the rocker panel, and any step on it is a bonus. Westin's XTS slider, for example, is 12-gauge steel channel with a 1-3/4 in tube and a small bolt-on step plate, and Westin says it protects the rocker panels from potential damage. If the truck stays on pavement and gravel, a board or drop step is more comfortable. If it sees ledges and ruts, a slider is the safer choice. Check the maker's instructions before you jack from any slider."),
 ("Are power running boards worth it on a Tacoma?",
  "They cost the most and keep the most clearance. RealTruck lists its VoltStep for the 2016–2023 Tacoma at $1,399.99, which is more than any fixed step here. In return the board tucks up when the doors close, so nothing fixed hangs under the rocker, and it drops to a 6 in wide step with LED lighting when a door opens. RealTruck recommends professional installation, and the kit adds motors, wiring and door switches that can fail. It suits a road truck that carries older passengers and still goes down a trail now and then. For hard off-road use, a fixed slider is simpler."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install running boards on a 2016–2023 Tacoma?",
  "Usually not, according to the makers. Westin says the HDX Stainless Drop and the XTS Rock Slider are bolt-on with no drilling, and that the wheel-to-wheel HDX Drop needs no drilling on most applications. RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB20 with no drilling, trimming or modifications, and the VoltStep power board with no drilling. Tyger says Star Armor bolts to the rocker panel with no drilling. TAC calls its Access Cab bar an easy bolt-on with all hardware included. If the brackets in your kit do not line up with the truck, stop and contact the seller. A kit that needs new holes is the wrong kit."),
 ("How much weight can Tacoma running boards hold?",
  "Only one maker on this page publishes a number in the text we read. Tyger quotes 500 lb per side for Star Armor. Westin's pages for the HDX Stainless Drop, the XTS Rock Slider and the wheel-to-wheel HDX Drop do not state a rating, RealTruck's pages for the Go Rhino RB20 and the VoltStep do not state one, and TAC's page does not either. A rating applies to one side and assumes a person stepping on it. It does not make a step a jacking point or a tie-down. If the number matters to you, ask the seller for it in writing before you order."),
 ("Will a wheel-to-wheel step fit my Double Cab?",
  "Only if the bed matches. A wheel-to-wheel bar runs past the cab to the rear wheel, so its length depends on the bed as well as the cab. Wikipedia lists the Double Cab with a 60.5 in bed as standard and a 73.7 in bed as an option. Westin's wheel-to-wheel HDX Drop 56-534185 is listed for the 2016–2023 Double Cab with the 73.7 in bed only, and the Amazon title calls it the 6 ft bed. Measure inside the bed at the rail from the bulkhead to the closed tailgate. We did not find a wheel-to-wheel listing for the short bed or for the Access Cab."),
 ("Do these steps fit the TRD Pro, TRD Off-Road and other grades?",
  "Wikipedia lists six grades for this generation: SR, SR5, TRD Off-Road, TRD Sport, Limited and TRD Pro, plus a Trail special edition. The makers we read sell steps by cab and model year, and none of the listing titles for our picks names a grade. That suggests one part per cab, but we could not confirm that every grade has free mounting points, or whether any grade left the factory with rock rails or steps in the way. For a TRD Pro, a Trail Edition or a lifted truck, send the seller your grade and cab and ask for a yes in writing. Lift kits also change the step height."),
]

